  "./corpus/case56_merged.txt": "Nursing Home Extension / Studiolada\r\n\r\nRetirement, Extension\r\n•\r\nVaucouleurs, France\r\nArchitects: Studiolada\r\nArea: \r\n470 m²\r\nYear: \r\n2018\r\nPhotographs:Ludmilla Cerveny\r\nManufacturers:  Carrieres de Savonnieres, Gerflor, Knauf, Sepalumic, Sylverwood\r\nLead Architect: Christophe Aubertin architecte dplg – studiolada\r\nCategory: Retirement, Extension\r\nAssociate Architect: Eleonore Nicolas architecte dplg – Studiolada\r\nStudiolada Collaborators: Guillaume Cecchin\r\nTechnical Consultants: Barthes Bois & Tecnos & Era & Abeco & Zeppelin\r\nWood Framework: Maddalon Frères & Houssard\r\nCommissioned By: EHPAD\r\nCity: Vaucouleurs\r\nCountry: France\r\n\r\nText description provided by the architects. Aging in the Lorraine region, and more precisely in the Meuse, in Vaucouleurs, nursing homes are not hospitals, and the time when these homes were over-sanitized and impersonal seems to be over. We were eager to suit the preferences of the occupants, who have a taste for noble materials, light, proportions and, to put it simply, architecture.\r\nThe building is situated on a slope. The view opens onto several layers of landscape, revealing the forgotten little wood and its tall trees. Inside the building, the large room is topped with a framework, which spans the entire width of the building and unfolds gradually towards the large bay window. On the south side, a walk-in garden allows residents to enjoy fresh air and sunshine.\r\nThe building structure is made of wood and covered with Meuse stone (“Savonnières”). Covering a flexible, expanding wooden structure with rigid stone is not a common practice — we had to make an ATEX request for the mounting system of the stone sheets (4 centimeters thick) into wooden frames with flexible joints. When you think about it, we are used to mounting sheets of glass only a few millimeters thick into wood frames...\r\nMaddalon produced two prototypes for us. We organized three resistance tests at the CRITT Bois Epinal, testing mechanical resistance to varying types of shock. These tests lead to the discovery of some weaknesses in the Savonnières stone, in certain configurations. Hence the use of a blend: 75% Meuse variety; 25% Burgundy variety.\r\nA “short-chain” research study was conducted: the architects (studiolada) and wood engineering (Barthes Bois), construction company (Maddalon). A preparatory meeting and a final commission took place at the CSTB in Marne-la-Vallée. Pages and pages of calculations, technical drawings, datasheets, and implementation protocols were needed to answer all the questions: What is the maximum expansion of wood, considering all configurations? Humidity, UV, temperature ...\r\nHow many millimeters should be left between the stone and the wood? What type of EPDM seal? Which guarantees, what resistance? What kind of hardware support should be used? How resistant will it be to corrosion? How will it react to the wind? How resistant are the stones to shocks, to bending, to shearing? What kind of solicitations will it endure from the public? How to manage the railings?\r\nHow will the stone and wood age, structurally and aesthetically? Which protocols for the choice and carving of the stones and the wood? Which Quality Assurance Plan for the implementation? What kind of maintenance? How will we replace a damaged stone in 15 years? How waterproof will it be? What type of rain screen? What surveillance measures for users?\r\nThe way forward is now clear: we now know how to dress wood buildings with mineral sheets (stones, concrete, ceramic, terracotta) at perfectly reasonable prices: half the price of stapled stone systems on concrete support. We only need to embrace the fixing system by making the wooden frame visible.\r\n\r\n========================================\r\n\r\nI. Vocabulary extraction\r\n- Plan (floor plan) and façades/elevations (vertical sections) shown\r\n- Linear building footprint\r\n- Central corridor / internal circulation spine\r\n- Repetitive rooms/units (modular bays)\r\n- Internal partition walls and doors\r\n- External galleries / balconies / walkways along both long façades\r\n- Ground-level arcade / colonnade (series of arches at the building base)\r\n- Entrances (main entrance at the bottom/arcade side)\r\n- Stair locations / vertical circulation (indicated on façades)\r\n- Windows / regular fenestration pattern\r\n- Structural elements: column/beam or regular bay structure (repetitive bay rhythm)\r\n- Site retaining/sloped ground adjacent to façades (shown as hatched slope in elevation)\r\n- Detail callouts (Detail 1–5) and scale bar\r\n\r\nII. Spatial relationship descriptions\r\n- Rooms/units are organized in a linear block around a continuous internal corridor that runs longitudinally, so units open off the central spine.\r\n- External galleries run parallel to the long faces of the block, forming a layered relationship: interior rooms → corridor → exterior gallery → sloped site/ground. These galleries provide semi-public circulation and transition between interior and exterior.\r\n- The ground-level arcade forms a pronounced, shared entrance/threshold space at one end of the plan, connecting the public exterior to the building interior and visually anchoring the base of the block.\r\n- Repetitive bays create a clear modular clustering: identical or mirrored unit modules are repeated along the length, producing rhythmic façade openings and structural regularity.\r\n- Vertical circulation (stair/landing) is aligned with the galleries, allowing access between floors without cutting through private rooms; the façades show the stairs and landings integrated into the external circulation system.\r\n- The building is read in layers: structural/vertical supports and arcade at the base, primary living modules in the middle, and continuous exterior walkways as a buffer to the sloped terrain flanking both sides.\r\n\r\nIII. Overall spatial impression (1–2 sentences)\r\nThe design uses a strict linear, modular logic: repeated unit bays accessed by a central corridor and flanked by continuous external galleries, with a prominent arcaded base forming the public threshold. The composition emphasizes repetitive rhythm, layered circulation, and a strong relationship between interior modules and exterior terraces/galleries.\r\n\r\nIV. Design intent reading\r\n- Efficiency and repetition: the plan’s modular, repetitive bays suggest an intent to achieve economical construction and clear structural/plan logic, likely for housing or communal accommodation.\r\n- Layered circulation and transitional space: by stacking internal corridor and external galleries, the design appears to prioritize breathable façades, cross-ventilation/ daylighting potential, and semi-public social interaction on the galleries.\r\n- Site response and base emphasis: the sloped hatched ground and the pronounced arcade at the base indicate a response to a sloping site and an intent to create a framed public entrance or communal forecourt.\r\n- Visual rhythm and tectonic clarity: regular fenestration, repeating bays, and clear detail callouts imply a concern for façade rhythm and readable construction/detailing. Overall the gesture balances private interior modules with shared, porous circulation and a strong ground-level identity.",
